package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q20 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter no: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        for (int i = 1; i <=n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=i; j++) {
                System.out.print("*");
            }
            for(int x = 0; x<2 *(n-i); x++){
                System.out.print(" ");
            }
            for (int j = 1; j <=i; j++) {
                System.out.print("*");
            }
            System.out.println();

        }
        for (int i = 1; i <n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=n-i ; j++) {
                System.out.print("*");
            }
            for (int x = 0; x < 2*i; x++) {
                System.out.print(" ");
            }
            for (int j = 1; j <= n-i ; j++) {
                System.out.print("*");
            }
            System.out.println();

        }


Approach

This pattern is known as the Butterfly Pattern. The pattern consists of two symmetric halves:

Upper Half

For each row i from 1 to n:

Print i stars on the left side.
Print 2 × (n - i) spaces in the middle.
Print i stars on the right side.

Example for n = 5:

*        *
**      **
***    ***
****  ****
**********
Lower Half

For each row i from 1 to n - 1:

Print (n - i) stars on the left side.
Print 2 × i spaces in the middle.
Print (n - i) stars on the right side.

Example:

****  ****
***    ***
**      **
*        *
Key Observation
Left and right star counts are always equal.
In the upper half:
Stars increase by 1 each row.
Spaces decrease by 2 each row.
In the lower half:
Stars decrease by 1 each row.
Spaces increase by 2 each row.
Time Complexity

There are 2n - 1 rows.

For each row, approximately 2n characters (stars + spaces) are printed.

Time Complexity: O(n²)
Space Complexity

Only a few loop variables are used, and no extra data structure is created.

Space Complexity: O(1)
